#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""zradio-change-watch — notices that a Z-Wave or Zigbee device is being added or removed, and syncs the standby five
quiet minutes later. Runs INSIDE both radio VMs as root (zradio-change-watch.service); only the ACTIVE ever sees
anything.

How it notices, with nothing but this VM: Home Assistant talks to the sticks over TCP to the floating address, ports
ZWAVE_PORT and ZIGBEE_PORT of /etc/zradio/zradio.conf. This watcher reads those packets as they arrive on the pair's
interface (a packet socket: it never connects to a port, never touches ser2net or a tty) and looks, in the direction
HOST -> STICK only, for the requests that change a network's membership:

  Z-Wave Serial API (frame: 01 LEN 00 FUNC … CHECKSUM, checksum verified)
    0x4A AddNodeToNetwork      mode 1-4 (any / controller / end node / existing) or 8 (SmartStart by home ID)
                               — not 5/6 (stop) and not 9 (the SmartStart listening the driver switches on at start)
    0x4B RemoveNodeFromNetwork mode 1-3 — not 5 (stop)
    0x61 RemoveFailedNode, 0x63 ReplaceFailedNode
  Z-Stack ZNP (frame: FE LEN CMD0 CMD1 DATA FCS, FCS verified) — only when radios.conf says ZIGBEE_FAMILY=znp
    25 36 ZDO_MGMT_PERMIT_JOIN_REQ with a duration > 0 (the network opened for joining; 0 = closed, ignored)
    25 34 ZDO_MGMT_LEAVE_REQ (a device told to leave)
  A Zigbee stick of another family is not parsed: its changes reach the standby with the nightly sync, if at all.

WHICH PACKETS COUNT. Anybody on the network can send this VM a packet that looks like one of Home Assistant's, so a
packet from outside proves nothing — not with a SYN before it either. What nobody outside can forge is what this VM's
own kernel SENDS. On a connection, every segment the kernel sends that is neither a SYN-ACK nor a reset carries "I
hold the host's bytes up to number N", and a kernel says that only on a connection whose handshake was completed (a
forged source address never gets that far) and only for bytes it took, in order, for ser2net. So the rule is: the
host's segments are kept, a few per connection, and READ ONLY AS FAR AS THIS VM'S KERNEL HAS ACKNOWLEDGED THEM, in the
order of their bytes. It follows that
  - a forged segment is never read: the kernel answers it with a reset, a SYN-ACK or the acknowledgment it gave before;
  - a connection that was open before the watcher started (no SYN seen) counts from its first request on: the request
    is kept, the kernel acknowledges it within a fraction of a second, and the first time the kernel is seen on a
    connection the kept segments that end exactly at its number are read;
  - a retransmission is read once, segments out of order are read in order, and a segment this socket was not handed
    (a hole) empties the parser's buffer: no frame is built across it.
A connection is (host address, host port, this VM's address, port). $MAX_STREAMS acknowledged connections are followed
(the one used longest ago goes first), and segments are kept for $MAX_UNPROVEN more that the kernel was not seen on
yet: a flood of forged segments can push out only those, at worst a new connection's very first request. A stream goes
when this VM sends a FIN, a reset or a SYN-ACK on it, or the host sends the one reset the kernel takes.
This needs the VM's answers to leave by the interface the requests arrive on. Where they do not (another route),
nothing can be counted and nothing is judged: one loud line says so, `result=loud blind port=…`.

A request seen = $STATE/change-seen is touched (on disk: a reboot inside the five minutes does not forget it) and a
journal line (one in $SEEN_LINE_EVERY s at most; the next one counts the rest). When change-seen is $QUIET s old and
this VM is the active: `zradio-sync run --reason device-change`, as a child — the packets are read while it runs.
  exit 0    a sync ran to the end: change-seen is dropped (a request seen DURING the sync keeps its own five minutes);
  exit 75   another sync is running, this one read nothing: change-seen stays, the start is not counted, and the
            watcher asks again $BUSY_RETRY s later;
  another   it failed: change-seen stays, the change is not on the standby. The watcher starts no second sync for the
            same request (each read is a pause for Home Assistant; a watcher that was restarted starts one more):
            zradio-sync's own pending mark makes its tick try again, and the nightly sync follows;
  no end in $SYNC_BOUND s: the sync and all it started get SIGTERM (SIGKILL $SYNC_KILL_AFTER s later) and one loud
            line; then as a failed one.
change-seen is also dropped, with no sync, when the last sync on record ($STATE/last-sync.json; its gen is the moment
its read began) read $QUIET s or more after the request: the nightly sync, a catch-up or the tick's retry did what
this one would do. A read that began INSIDE the five minutes does not count: the device may have joined after it.
At most $MAX_PER_DAY device-change syncs start in 24 h (each is a pause for Home Assistant): past that, one loud line
and the nightly sync covers it. Over-triggering (an inclusion started and canceled) costs one sync; a missed request
costs nothing the nightly sync does not repair.

A second job, with the same packets: A STICK THAT STOPPED ANSWERING while its tty is still there (hung firmware). The
health check of keepalived only asks whether the ttys exist, so such a stick would be served for ever. Here: every
request Home Assistant sends to a port (Z-Wave payloads of 2+ bytes — its one-byte ACKs do not count; ZNP frames) is
counted until the stick sends anything back. $WEDGE_MIN or more requests unanswered, the first of them $WEDGE_AFTER s
ago, on the VM that serves = the stick is declared wedged: $STATE/stick-wedged is written and one loud line;
zradio-serve check fails on that file, keepalived hands the radios to the other VM within seconds. The file stays until
the sticks answer a probe again (zradio-serve hush, at every BACKUP and every 15 min). At most $WEDGE_MAX_PER_DAY
declarations in 24 h per VM: if both VMs' sticks "stop answering" the cause is not a stick.
The requests are those of "which packets count" above; the seconds are counted on a clock that never steps (a time
sync that moves the wall clock makes no silence).
Two guards, from a real outage (2026-11-11: a first version could not see the sticks' answers, called two healthy
sticks silent in turn, and no VM served for 2 min 22 s): a stick is judged only after this process has seen it answer
at least once, and at most $WEDGE_MAX_PER_DAY declarations are made in a day. The check is on; there is no setting for
it. (ZRADIO_CHANGE_WEDGE=observe in the unit's environment, a drop-in, puts it back to logging `result=would-wedge`
only: for looking at a pair whose check misfires.) What brings a silent stick back is zradio-serve hush, on the VM
that gave the radios up.

The conf is read through zradio-common.sh, as every shell tool of the kit reads it (the same checks, the same defaults).
NOTHING TO WATCH (radios.conf names no radio, no interface holds SELF, a conf that does not load): the service does
not exit — its unit restarts whatever exits, every 5 s, for ever. It says so in one journal line (`result=waiting`),
looks again every $LOOK_EVERY s and goes on by itself when there is something to watch.

  zradio-change-watch run            the service
  The tests' doors (no socket is opened):
  zradio-change-watch parse-test     stdin: lines "zwave|zigbee <hex bytes>" (host->stick payloads) -> the events
  zradio-change-watch segment-test   stdin: one IPv4 packet in hex per line -> what tcp_segment reads from it
  zradio-change-watch stream-test    stdin, with <t> in seconds of the clock that never steps:
                                       "<t> in|out <from addr:port> <to addr:port> <flags: letters of SAFRP> <seq> <ack> [payload hex]"
                                                         a segment that arrived / that this VM sent
                                       "<t> check"       the look the service takes every CHECK_EVERY s
                                       "<t> clock <s>"   the wall clock steps by <s> seconds
                                       "<t> state"       -> "streams=N unproven=M"
                                     -> the journal lines. Ports 6639 (Z-Wave) and 6638 (ZNP).
  zradio-change-watch wedge-test     stdin: lines "<t> host <zwave|zigbee> <bytes>", "<t> stick <kind>", "<t> check"
                                     -> what a check at <t> would declare (the counting)
  zradio-change-watch declare-test <kind> <count> <age>   the declaration (marker, limit, line)
  zradio-change-watch check-test [S] the five-quiet-minutes check (maybe_sync), 5 times a second, until the sync it
                                     started has ended and S seconds have passed (default 0: one check and its sync)
  zradio-change-watch conf-test      the ports, the interface and the parsers this VM would use, as one line
  zradio-change-watch wait-test      the same line, after waiting as the service does while there is nothing to watch

Journal lines: `zradio: event=change …`. Tests: tests/t-change-watch.sh. Env: ZRADIO_CHANGE_*, ZRADIO_CONF, ZRADIO_RADIOS,
ZRADIO_LIB.
"""
import calendar, json, os, signal, socket, struct, subprocess, sys, time

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
LIB = os.environ.get("ZRADIO_LIB") or os.path.join(HERE, "..", "lib")
if not os.path.exists(os.path.join(LIB, "zradio-common.sh")): LIB = "/usr/local/lib/zradio"
STATE = os.environ.get("ZRADIO_CHANGE_STATE", "/var/lib/zradio")
RUN = os.environ.get("ZRADIO_CHANGE_RUN", "/run/zradio")
SYNC = os.environ.get("ZRADIO_CHANGE_SYNC", "/usr/local/sbin/zradio-sync")
LOGGER = os.environ.get("ZRADIO_CHANGE_LOGGER", "logger")
QUIET = int(os.environ.get("ZRADIO_CHANGE_QUIET", "300"))
MAX_PER_DAY = int(os.environ.get("ZRADIO_CHANGE_MAX_PER_DAY", "6"))
BUSY_RETRY = int(os.environ.get("ZRADIO_CHANGE_BUSY_RETRY", "60"))       # s until a sync that found another one running is asked for again
SYNC_BOUND = int(os.environ.get("ZRADIO_CHANGE_SYNC_BOUND", "2700"))     # s: zradio-sync's own worst case is about 25 min (hello, the
                                                                        # read, three sends of 7 min each, the pauses between them)
SYNC_KILL_AFTER = 30             # s between the SIGTERM at the bound and the SIGKILL
BUSY = 75                        # zradio-sync run's exit code for "another sync is running: this call read nothing"
LOOK_EVERY = int(os.environ.get("ZRADIO_CHANGE_LOOK_EVERY", "60"))       # s between two looks while there is nothing to watch
WEDGE_AFTER = int(os.environ.get("ZRADIO_CHANGE_WEDGE_AFTER", "180"))
WEDGE_MIN = int(os.environ.get("ZRADIO_CHANGE_WEDGE_MIN", "5"))
WEDGE_MAX_PER_DAY = int(os.environ.get("ZRADIO_CHANGE_WEDGE_MAX_PER_DAY", "2"))
WEDGE_TRACE = os.environ.get("ZRADIO_CHANGE_WEDGE_TRACE") == "1"     # print the worst unanswered run seen, change nothing
WEDGE_MODE = os.environ.get("ZRADIO_CHANGE_WEDGE", "act")     # act: write the marker (keepalived then hands over);
                                                                    # observe: say what would be declared, change nothing
LOG_MAX = 400                    # the same cut as zradio-common.sh's ZR_LOG_MAX
DAY = 86400                      # s: both limits count what happened in the last 24 h
LIMIT_LINE_EVERY = 21600         # s: the "limit reached" line is said at most every 6 h
SEEN_LINE_EVERY = 10             # s: one "seen" line at most; the next one counts the requests in between
CHECK_EVERY = 5                  # s between two looks at the quiet mark and the unanswered counts
MAX_STREAMS = 16                 # acknowledged connections followed at once: Home Assistant holds one per port, a few stale ones
MAX_UNPROVEN = 64                # connections whose segments are kept until this VM's kernel is seen on them (forged ones end here)
WINDOW = 65536                   # bytes past the kernel's acknowledgment in which a host segment is kept: the unacknowledged ones
                                 # of a serial line are a few hundred bytes; farther off is no part of this connection
HELD_SEGS = 64                   # segments kept per connection, and WINDOW bytes; the oldest dropped first
BUF_MAX, BUF_KEEP = 4096, 64     # a stream buffer that grew this long without a frame keeps only its tail
BLIND_MIN, BLIND_AFTER = 20, 60  # segments to a port, over this many s, with nothing seen leaving from it: said loudly
SEQ_MASK, SEQ_HALF = 0xFFFFFFFF, 0x7FFFFFFF     # TCP sequence numbers wrap at 2^32; "ahead" is within half of that
ETH_P_ALL = 0x0003               # every protocol: only such a socket is also handed the packets this VM SENDS
ETH_P_IP = 0x0800                # IPv4 (ARP, VRRP's own frames and the rest pass by)
IPV4_HEADER_MIN = TCP_HEADER_MIN = 20     # bytes, without options
TCP_FLAGS = {"F": 0x01, "S": 0x02, "R": 0x04, "P": 0x08, "A": 0x10}
TCP_FIN, TCP_SYN, TCP_RST, TCP_ACK = TCP_FLAGS["F"], TCP_FLAGS["S"], TCP_FLAGS["R"], TCP_FLAGS["A"]
PORTS = {}                       # port -> "zwave" | "zigbee": setup() fills it from the conf
RADIOS = "radios.conf"           # its path, as zradio-common.sh found it (setup)
MIN_REQUEST = {"zwave": 2, "zigbee": 5}          # a one-byte Z-Wave ACK/NAK/CAN asks for no answer; a ZNP frame is 5+ bytes
ZIGBEE_PARSED = True                             # False for a Zigbee family whose frames this watcher does not know
SEEN = os.path.join(STATE, "change-seen")
STARTS = os.path.join(STATE, "change-syncs")      # one epoch per line: the device-change syncs started in the last 24 h
LAST = os.path.join(STATE, "last-sync.json")      # zradio-sync's record of the last sync the standby confirmed
WEDGED = os.path.join(STATE, "stick-wedged")      # zradio-serve check fails while this exists; zradio-serve hush clears it
WEDGE_LOG = os.path.join(STATE, "wedge-declared") # one epoch per line: the declarations of the last 24 h


def log(text):
    print(text, flush=True)
    try: subprocess.run([LOGGER, "-t", "zradio", "event=change " + text[:LOG_MAX]], timeout=5)
    except Exception: pass


class ZWave:
    """Host -> controller byte stream: Serial API frames, checksum-verified."""
    SOF, REQUEST = 0x01, 0x00
    ADD, REMOVE = 0x4A, 0x4B
    NAMES = {ADD: "add-node", REMOVE: "remove-node", 0x61: "remove-failed-node", 0x63: "replace-failed-node"}
    MODES = {ADD: (1, 2, 3, 4, 8), REMOVE: (1, 2, 3)}       # the modes that start an inclusion / exclusion

    def __init__(self): self.buf = bytearray()

    def feed(self, data):
        out = []; b = self.buf; b += data
        while b:
            if b[0] != self.SOF: del b[0]; continue       # ACK/NAK/CAN and anything between frames
            if len(b) < 2: break
            ln = b[1]
            if ln < 3: del b[0]; continue                 # not a frame start
            if len(b) < ln + 2: break                     # the rest comes with the next packet
            frame = bytes(b[:ln + 2])
            cs = 0xFF
            for x in frame[1:-1]: cs ^= x
            if cs != frame[-1]: del b[0]; continue        # a 0x01 inside other bytes: resync one byte on
            del b[:ln + 2]
            typ, func = frame[2], frame[3]
            if typ != self.REQUEST or func not in self.NAMES: continue
            if func in self.MODES:
                mode = frame[4] & 0x0F if len(frame) > 5 else 0
                if mode in self.MODES[func]: out.append("zwave %s mode=%d" % (self.NAMES[func], mode))
            else:
                out.append("zwave %s" % self.NAMES[func])
        if len(b) > BUF_MAX: del b[:-BUF_KEEP]
        return out


class Zigbee:
    """Host -> coordinator byte stream: ZNP frames, FCS-verified."""
    SOF, MAX_LEN = 0xFE, 250
    ZDO_SREQ, PERMIT_JOIN, LEAVE = 0x25, 0x36, 0x34

    def __init__(self): self.buf = bytearray()

    def feed(self, data):
        out = []; b = self.buf; b += data
        while b:
            if b[0] != self.SOF: del b[0]; continue
            if len(b) < 2: break
            ln = b[1]
            if ln > self.MAX_LEN: del b[0]; continue
            if len(b) < ln + 5: break
            frame = bytes(b[:ln + 5])
            fcs = 0
            for x in frame[1:-1]: fcs ^= x
            if fcs != frame[-1]: del b[0]; continue
            del b[:ln + 5]
            cmd0, cmd1, data_ = frame[2], frame[3], frame[4:-1]
            if cmd0 != self.ZDO_SREQ: continue
            if cmd1 == self.PERMIT_JOIN and len(data_) >= 4:
                dur = data_[3]
                if dur > 0: out.append("zigbee permit-join duration=%d" % dur)
            elif cmd1 == self.LEAVE:
                out.append("zigbee leave-request")
        if len(b) > BUF_MAX: del b[:-BUF_KEEP]
        return out


class Unparsed:
    """A Zigbee family whose serial protocol this watcher does not read: bytes are counted (the silent-stick check),
    no add/remove request is ever seen."""

    def __init__(self): self.buf = bytearray()

    def feed(self, data): return []


def parser(kind):
    if kind == "zwave": return ZWave()
    return Zigbee() if ZIGBEE_PARSED else Unparsed()


def tcp_segment(pkt):
    """An IPv4 packet -> (source address, destination address, source port, destination port, sequence number,
    acknowledgment number, TCP flags, payload); None when it is not a whole TCP segment. The payload ends where the IP
    header's total length says (a short Ethernet frame carries padding after it)."""
    if len(pkt) < IPV4_HEADER_MIN + TCP_HEADER_MIN or pkt[9] != socket.IPPROTO_TCP: return None
    ihl = (pkt[0] & 0x0F) * 4
    if len(pkt) < ihl + TCP_HEADER_MIN: return None
    total = struct.unpack("!H", pkt[2:4])[0]
    sport, dport, seq, ack = struct.unpack("!HHII", pkt[ihl:ihl + 12])
    flags, off = pkt[ihl + 13], (pkt[ihl + 12] >> 4) * 4
    return pkt[12:16], pkt[16:20], sport, dport, seq, ack, flags, pkt[ihl + off:total]


class Stream:
    """One connection's host -> stick bytes, read only as far as this VM's kernel has acknowledged them ("which packets
    count" at the top)."""

    def __init__(self, kind):
        self.kind = kind; self.parser = parser(kind)
        self.at = None       # the number of the first host byte the kernel has not acknowledged yet; None: the kernel was
                             # not seen on this connection yet
        self.segs = {}       # sequence number -> payload: the host's segments the kernel has not acknowledged yet

    def host(self, seq, payload):
        """A segment from the host: kept until the kernel acknowledges its bytes."""
        if self.at is not None:
            behind = (self.at - seq) & SEQ_MASK
            if 0 < behind < len(payload): seq, payload = self.at, payload[behind:]     # a retransmission that carries new bytes too
            if ((seq - self.at) & SEQ_MASK) >= WINDOW: return      # read already, or nowhere near what the kernel expects
        self.segs.pop(seq, None); self.segs[seq] = payload         # the same bytes again: the later ones, as an in-order
                                                                   # segment replaces what the kernel held out of order
        room = WINDOW + len(payload)                               # (this one always fits, whatever its size)
        while len(self.segs) > HELD_SEGS or sum(len(p) for p in self.segs.values()) > room:
            self.segs.pop(next(iter(self.segs)))

    def end(self):
        """The number after the last kept byte that follows the acknowledged ones without a hole: what the kernel
        expects next, when it took all of them."""
        e = self.at
        while e in self.segs: e = (e + len(self.segs[e])) & SEQ_MASK
        return e

    def acked(self, ack):
        """The kernel sent `ack`, the number of the next host byte it expects: the kept segments below it are read, in
        the order of their bytes. Returns (the add/remove requests in them, the sizes of the segments read)."""
        events, sizes = [], []
        if self.at is None:                  # the first sight of the kernel here: back over the kept segments that end
            self.at = ack                    # exactly at its number (a forged one cannot know where that is)
            ends = {(s + len(p)) & SEQ_MASK: s for s, p in self.segs.items()}
            while self.at in ends: self.at = ends.pop(self.at)
        n = (ack - self.at) & SEQ_MASK
        if n > SEQ_HALF: return events, sizes                      # an older acknowledgment, late: nothing new
        while n:
            p = self.segs.pop(self.at, None)
            if p is None:                    # a hole (a segment this socket was not handed; the number a FIN takes):
                self.parser.buf.clear()      # on to the nearest kept segment below ack, and no frame across it
                step = min((d for d in ((s - self.at) & SEQ_MASK for s in self.segs) if d < n), default=n)
            else:
                if len(p) > n: self.segs.setdefault(ack, p[n:]); p = p[:n]         # acknowledged in part
                events += self.parser.feed(p); sizes.append(len(p)); step = len(p)
            self.at = (self.at + step) & SEQ_MASK; n -= step
        for s in [s for s in self.segs if ((s - ack) & SEQ_MASK) >= WINDOW]: del self.segs[s]     # what lies behind now
        return events, sizes


class Wedge:
    """Requests from the host that a stick has not answered: per kind, how many and since when."""

    def __init__(self, after=None, need=None):
        self.after = WEDGE_AFTER if after is None else after; self.need = WEDGE_MIN if need is None else need
        self.count = {}; self.first = {}; self.worst = {}; self.heard = set()

    def host(self, kind, now, nbytes):
        if nbytes < MIN_REQUEST[kind]: return
        if not self.count.get(kind): self.first[kind] = now
        self.count[kind] = self.count.get(kind, 0) + 1

    def stick(self, kind, now=None):
        if now is not None: self.heard.add(kind)      # this process has seen this stick answer: only then can silence mean anything
        if now is not None and self.count.get(kind, 0) >= self.need:
            self.worst[kind] = max(self.worst.get(kind, 0), now - self.first[kind])
        self.count[kind] = 0; self.first.pop(kind, None)

    def reset(self): self.count.clear(); self.first.clear()

    def wedged(self, now):
        return [(k, n, now - self.first[k]) for k, n in sorted(self.count.items())
                if k in self.heard and n >= self.need and now - self.first[k] >= self.after]


def epochs_last_day(path, now):
    """The epochs a file holds (one per line) that are less than a day old."""
    try: xs = [float(x) for x in open(path).read().split()]
    except Exception: xs = []
    return [x for x in xs if now - x < DAY]


def write_epochs(path, xs):
    open(path + ".tmp", "w").write("\n".join("%d" % x for x in xs) + "\n"); os.replace(path + ".tmp", path)


def role():
    try: return open(os.path.join(RUN, "role")).read().split()[0]
    except Exception: return "none"


def serving():
    return subprocess.run(["systemctl", "is-active", "--quiet", "ser2net"]).returncode == 0


def declare_wedged(now, kind, count, age):
    """The active gives the radios up: the marker zradio-serve check fails on, one loud line. False when held back."""
    if not (role() == "MASTER" and serving()): return False
    if WEDGE_MODE != "act":
        log("result=would-wedge stick=%s — %d requests unanswered for %d s; observe mode (ZRADIO_CHANGE_WEDGE=observe "
            "in the unit's environment): nothing is changed" % (kind, count, age))
        return False
    xs = epochs_last_day(WEDGE_LOG, now)
    if len(xs) >= WEDGE_MAX_PER_DAY:
        log("result=loud wedged-not-declared stick=%s — %d requests unanswered for %d s, but this VM declared a wedged "
            "stick %d times in the last 24 h (the limit): the cause may not be a stick; nothing is changed"
            % (kind, count, age, len(xs)))
        return False
    write_epochs(WEDGE_LOG, xs + [now])
    open(WEDGED + ".tmp", "w").write("%s %s\n" % (kind, time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(now))))
    os.replace(WEDGED + ".tmp", WEDGED)
    log("result=loud wedged stick=%s — Home Assistant sent %d requests in %d s and the stick answered nothing, with "
        "its tty still there: this VM gives the radios up (%s); the other VM takes over. zradio-serve hush "
        "now tries to bring the stick back (a soft reset, a USB re-bind, then its power through the node)" % (kind, count, age, WEDGED))
    return True


def last_read():
    """The moment the last sync on record began its read, as an epoch: the gen of zradio-sync's last-sync.json (UTC, to
    the second). None when there is no record that reads."""
    try: return calendar.timegm(time.strptime(json.load(open(LAST))["gen"], "%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ"))
    except Exception: return None


def drop_mark(mark=None):
    """change-seen removed — with a mark: only when no request touched it since."""
    try:
        if mark is None or os.stat(SEEN).st_mtime == mark: os.unlink(SEEN)
    except OSError: pass


def sync_ended(now, mono, state):
    """The sync this watcher started, looked at again. False while it runs (past its bound it is ended); True once
    its exit code is read: 0 drops the mark, BUSY asks again later, another keeps the mark and asks no more for it."""
    child = state["child"]; rc = child.poll(); ran = mono - state["since"]
    if rc is None:
        if ran >= SYNC_BOUND and (not state["told"] or ran >= SYNC_BOUND + SYNC_KILL_AFTER):
            # The whole group: the sync's ssh, tar and readers end with it; bash runs its EXIT trap on SIGTERM, so a
            # read under way still gives the sticks back (what systemd does to the nightly unit at its own timeout).
            try: os.killpg(child.pid, signal.SIGKILL if state["told"] else signal.SIGTERM)
            except OSError: pass
            if not state["told"]:
                state["told"] = True
                log("result=loud zradio-sync run did not end in %d s: it and all it started are ended (SIGTERM; SIGKILL "
                    "%d s later)" % (SYNC_BOUND, SYNC_KILL_AFTER))
        return False
    mark = state["mark"]; state["child"] = None
    if rc == 0:
        drop_mark(mark)                                         # a request seen DURING the sync keeps its own five minutes
        log("result=synced zradio-sync run ended rc=0")
    elif rc == BUSY:
        xs = epochs_last_day(STARTS, now)
        if state["started"] in xs: xs.remove(state["started"]); write_epochs(STARTS, xs)       # it read nothing: no pause, not counted
        if state.get("busy") != mark:
            log("result=busy zradio-sync run ended rc=%d: another sync is running, this one read nothing — the mark "
                "stays, asked again every %d s until one ends" % (rc, BUSY_RETRY))
        state["busy"] = mark; state["again"] = mono + BUSY_RETRY
    else:
        state["failed"] = mark
        log("result=sync-failed zradio-sync run ended rc=%d — the mark stays: the change is not on the standby. No second "
            "sync from here for the same request (zradio-sync's pending mark makes its tick try again; the nightly sync "
            "follows)" % rc)
    return True


def maybe_sync(now, mono, state):
    """change-seen old enough -> one sync, started as a child and looked at again at every call: nothing here waits for
    it. now: the wall clock (the marks on disk); mono: a clock that never steps (the waits). state: what one call
    leaves for the next — the child and its mark, the marks a sync failed or was busy for, the last limit line."""
    if state.get("child") is not None and not sync_ended(now, mono, state): return
    try: mark = os.stat(SEEN).st_mtime
    except OSError: return
    age = now - mark
    if age < QUIET: return
    if not (role() == "MASTER" and serving()):
        drop_mark()
        log("result=dropped this VM is not the active any more: the mark is dropped (the new active's sync covers it)")
        return
    read = last_read()
    if read is not None and read >= int(mark) + QUIET:          # whole seconds on both sides: gen has no fractions
        drop_mark(mark)
        log("result=covered the last sync on record began its read %d s after the last add/remove request: it holds "
            "the change, no sync from here" % (read - int(mark)))
        return
    if state.get("failed") == mark: return
    if state.get("busy") == mark and mono < state["again"]: return
    xs = epochs_last_day(STARTS, now)
    if len(xs) >= MAX_PER_DAY:
        if state.get("warned") is None or mono - state["warned"] > LIMIT_LINE_EVERY:
            state["warned"] = mono
            log("result=loud %d device-change syncs started in the last 24 h (the limit): no more until one ages out "
                "— the nightly sync covers the change" % len(xs))
        return
    write_epochs(STARTS, xs + [now])
    if state.get("busy") != mark:
        log("result=sync %d s quiet since the last add/remove request: zradio-sync run --reason device-change" % age)
    # Its own session: the bound's signal reaches the sync and all it started, and nothing else.
    state.update(child=subprocess.Popen([SYNC, "run", "--reason", "device-change"], stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                                        stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, start_new_session=True),
                 since=mono, mark=mark, started=int(now), told=False)


# zradio.conf and radios.conf as the shell tools read them: zradio-common.sh's checks and defaults, one value per line.
CONF_SH = r'''. "$1/zradio-common.sh" || exit 2
zr_load_conf || { echo "$ZR_ERR" >&2; exit 1; }
zr_load_radios || { ZWAVE=none ZIGBEE=none ZIGBEE_FAMILY=""; }
zr_iface || { echo "$ZR_ERR" >&2; exit 1; }
printf '%s\n' "$ZR_IFACE" "$ZWAVE_PORT" "$ZIGBEE_PORT" "$ZWAVE" "$ZIGBEE" "$ZIGBEE_FAMILY" "$ZR_RADIOS"'''


class ConfError(Exception):
    """The conf did not load, or no interface holds SELF: zradio-common.sh's own words."""


def setup():
    """The ports, the Zigbee parser and the interface, from zradio.conf and radios.conf. Returns the interface name."""
    global PORTS, ZIGBEE_PARSED, RADIOS
    r = subprocess.run(["bash", "-c", CONF_SH, "zradio-change-watch", LIB], capture_output=True, text=True)
    if r.returncode != 0: raise ConfError(r.stderr.strip() or "the conf did not load")
    dev, zw_port, zb_port, zwave, zigbee, zigbee_family, RADIOS = r.stdout.split("\n")[:7]
    PORTS = {}
    if zwave != "none": PORTS[int(zw_port)] = "zwave"
    if zigbee != "none": PORTS[int(zb_port)] = "zigbee"
    ZIGBEE_PARSED = zigbee_family == "znp"
    MIN_REQUEST["zigbee"] = 5 if ZIGBEE_PARSED else 1
    return dev


def wait_setup():
    """setup(), tried again every LOOK_EVERY s while there is nothing to watch. Never an exit: the unit restarts
    whatever exits (Restart=always), so "nothing to watch" would be a restart every 5 s for ever. One journal line per
    reason, however long it lasts."""
    said = None
    while True:
        try: dev = setup(); why = None if PORTS else "%s names no radio" % RADIOS
        except ConfError as e: why = str(e)
        if why is None: return dev
        if why != said:
            said = why; log("result=waiting %s — nothing to watch; looking again every %d s" % (why, LOOK_EVERY))
        time.sleep(LOOK_EVERY)


class Watch:
    """What the service does with every segment and at every look; the tests drive it with clocks of their own
    (stream-test). now: the wall clock (the marks on disk); mono: a clock that never steps (ages and waits)."""

    def __init__(self):
        self.streams = {}; self.unproven = {}     # (host address, host port, this VM's address, port) -> Stream: the
                                                  # connections the kernel was seen on (used last = last), and not yet
        self.wedge = Wedge(); self.state = {}; self.last_check = None
        self.said = None; self.unsaid = 0; self.unsaid_last = ""        # the "seen" lines: one in SEEN_LINE_EVERY s
        self.sent = set(); self.blind = {}        # the ports this VM was seen sending from; port -> [segments to it, since, said]

    def packet(self, seg, outgoing, mono):
        src, dst, sport, dport, seq, ack, flags, payload = seg
        if outgoing:                                  # what this VM sends: its kernel's word on a connection, a stick's bytes
            if sport not in PORTS: return
            key = (dst, dport, src, sport); self.sent.add(sport); self.blind.pop(sport, None)
            st = self.streams.pop(key, None) or self.unproven.pop(key, None) or Stream(PORTS[sport])
            if flags & TCP_ACK and not flags & (TCP_SYN | TCP_RST):
                self.read(st, st.acked(ack), mono)
                if not flags & TCP_FIN:               # (a FIN: ser2net closed it — all the host sent is read by now)
                    self.streams[key] = st
                    if len(self.streams) > MAX_STREAMS: self.streams.pop(next(iter(self.streams)))
            # else a SYN-ACK (a new connection begins on this address and port) or a reset: the old stream is gone
            if payload: self.wedge.stick(PORTS[sport], mono)
        elif dport in PORTS:                          # what somebody sends to a stick's port: kept, not read yet
            key = (src, sport, dst, dport); st = self.streams.get(key)
            if st is not None and flags & TCP_RST:
                if seq == st.end():                   # the one reset the kernel takes: at the very next byte's number
                    self.read(st, st.acked(seq), mono); del self.streams[key]
                return
            if not payload: return
            if dport not in self.sent:
                b = self.blind.setdefault(dport, [0, mono, False]); b[0] += 1
            if st is None:
                st = self.unproven.get(key)
                if st is None:
                    st = self.unproven[key] = Stream(PORTS[dport])
                    if len(self.unproven) > MAX_UNPROVEN: self.unproven.pop(next(iter(self.unproven)))
            st.host(seq, payload)

    def read(self, st, got, mono):
        """Bytes the kernel acknowledged were read: the requests count for the silent-stick check, an add/remove
        request is a mark and a line."""
        events, sizes = got
        for n in sizes: self.wedge.host(st.kind, mono, n)
        if not events: return
        open(SEEN, "a").close(); os.utime(SEEN, None)
        for ev in events:
            if self.said is None or mono - self.said >= SEEN_LINE_EVERY:
                self.said = mono; log("result=seen %s — the standby is synced after %d quiet s" % (ev, QUIET))
            else: self.unsaid += 1; self.unsaid_last = ev

    def tick(self, now, mono):
        """The look at the quiet mark, the unanswered counts and what was not said yet: every CHECK_EVERY s."""
        if self.last_check is not None and mono - self.last_check < CHECK_EVERY: return
        self.last_check = mono
        if self.unsaid and mono - self.said >= SEEN_LINE_EVERY:
            log("result=seen %d more add/remove requests in the last %d s (the last: %s)"
                % (self.unsaid, mono - self.said, self.unsaid_last))
            self.said = mono; self.unsaid = 0
        try: maybe_sync(now, mono, self.state)
        except Exception as e: log("result=error %s: %s" % (type(e).__name__, str(e)[:120]))
        for port, b in sorted(self.blind.items()):
            if not b[2] and b[0] >= BLIND_MIN and mono - b[1] >= BLIND_AFTER:
                b[2] = True
                log("result=loud blind port=%d — %d segments to it arrived in %d s and nothing this VM sends from it was "
                    "seen on this interface (its answers leave by another one?): no add/remove request can be counted, "
                    "no silent stick judged" % (port, b[0], mono - b[1]))
        wedge = self.wedge
        try:
            if WEDGE_TRACE:
                print("trace: heard %s, unanswered now %s, worst answered run %s"
                      % (sorted(wedge.heard), dict(wedge.count), {k: round(v, 1) for k, v in wedge.worst.items()}),
                      flush=True)
            elif not (role() == "MASTER" and serving()): wedge.reset()       # only a VM that serves can have a wedged stick
            else:
                for kind, count, age in wedge.wedged(mono):
                    declare_wedged(now, kind, count, age); wedge.stick(kind)
        except Exception as e: log("result=error wedge %s: %s" % (type(e).__name__, str(e)[:120]))


def run():
    os.makedirs(STATE, exist_ok=True)
    dev = wait_setup()
    # ETH_P_ALL, not ETH_P_IP: the kernel hands the packets this machine SENDS only to all-protocol packet sockets. With
    # ETH_P_IP the sticks' answers were never seen, every request looked unanswered, and both VMs declared a healthy
    # stick wedged in turn (the outage of 2026-11-11: 2 min 22 s with no radio VM serving).
    s = socket.socket(socket.AF_PACKET, socket.SOCK_DGRAM, socket.htons(ETH_P_ALL))    # link header stripped; receive only
    s.bind((dev, ETH_P_ALL)); s.settimeout(CHECK_EVERY)
    log("result=started watching %s for add/remove requests to ports %s%s (quiet %d s, at most %d syncs a day); "
        "silent sticks: %s after %d requests unanswered for %d s"
        % (dev, "/".join("%d (%s)" % (p, PORTS[p]) for p in sorted(PORTS)),
           "" if ZIGBEE_PARSED or "zigbee" not in PORTS.values() else "; this Zigbee family is not parsed",
           QUIET, MAX_PER_DAY, WEDGE_MODE, WEDGE_MIN, WEDGE_AFTER))
    w = Watch()
    while True:
        try: pkt, addr = s.recvfrom(65535)
        except TimeoutError: pkt = None
        # PACKET_HOST and PACKET_OUTGOING: what is addressed to this VM and what it sends. A frame for another machine
        # (an interface somebody set promiscuous) is none of this kernel's connections.
        if pkt is not None and addr[1] == ETH_P_IP and addr[2] in (socket.PACKET_HOST, socket.PACKET_OUTGOING):
            seg = tcp_segment(pkt)
            if seg: w.packet(seg, addr[2] == socket.PACKET_OUTGOING, time.monotonic())
        w.tick(time.time(), time.monotonic())


def parse_test():
    p = {"zwave": ZWave(), "zigbee": Zigbee()}
    for line in sys.stdin:
        line = line.strip()
        if not line or line.startswith("#"): continue
        kind, _, hexs = line.partition(" ")
        for ev in p[kind].feed(bytes.fromhex(hexs)): print(ev)
    return 0


def segment_test():
    for line in sys.stdin:
        line = line.strip()
        if not line or line.startswith("#"): continue
        seg = tcp_segment(bytes.fromhex(line))
        if seg is None: print("not a TCP segment"); continue
        src, dst, sport, dport, seq, ack, flags, payload = seg
        print("src=%s dst=%s sport=%d dport=%d seq=%d ack=%d flags=0x%02x payload=%s"
              % (socket.inet_ntoa(src), socket.inet_ntoa(dst), sport, dport, seq, ack, flags, payload.hex()))
    return 0


def stream_test():
    global PORTS
    PORTS = {6639: "zwave", 6638: "zigbee"}
    w = Watch(); wall = time.time()                  # the wall clock: this start + <t> + the steps of "clock" lines
    for line in sys.stdin:
        f = line.split()
        if not f or f[0].startswith("#"): continue
        t = float(f[0])
        if f[1] == "clock": wall += float(f[2])
        elif f[1] == "check": w.tick(wall + t, t)
        elif f[1] == "state": print("streams=%d unproven=%d" % (len(w.streams), len(w.unproven)), flush=True)
        else:
            (sa, sp), (da, dp) = f[2].split(":"), f[3].split(":")
            flags = 0
            for c in f[4]: flags |= TCP_FLAGS[c]
            w.packet((socket.inet_aton(sa), socket.inet_aton(da), int(sp), int(dp), int(f[5]), int(f[6]), flags,
                      bytes.fromhex(f[7]) if len(f) > 7 else b""), f[1] == "out", t)
    return 0


def wedge_test():
    w = Wedge()
    for line in sys.stdin:
        f = line.split()
        if not f or f[0].startswith("#"): continue
        t = float(f[0])
        if f[1] == "host": w.host(f[2], t, int(f[3]))
        elif f[1] == "stick": w.stick(f[2], t)
        elif f[1] == "check":
            got = w.wedged(t)
            print("%g: %s" % (t, "; ".join("%s wedged, %d unanswered for %d s" % (k, n, a) for k, n, a in got) or "nothing"))
    return 0


def check_test(secs):
    state = {}; t0 = time.monotonic()
    while True:
        maybe_sync(time.time(), time.monotonic(), state)
        if state.get("child") is None and time.monotonic() - t0 >= secs: return 0
        if state.get("child") is not None: print("pass: the sync is running", flush=True)
        time.sleep(0.2)


def conf_test(look):
    try: dev = look()
    except ConfError as e: raise SystemExit("zradio-change-watch: %s" % e)
    print("iface=%s ports=%s zigbee_parsed=%s"
          % (dev, ",".join("%d:%s" % (p, PORTS[p]) for p in sorted(PORTS)), ZIGBEE_PARSED))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    mode = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else ""
    if mode == "run": run()
    elif mode == "parse-test": sys.exit(parse_test())
    elif mode == "segment-test": sys.exit(segment_test())
    elif mode == "stream-test": sys.exit(stream_test())
    elif mode == "wedge-test": sys.exit(wedge_test())
    elif mode == "declare-test": print(declare_wedged(time.time(), sys.argv[2], int(sys.argv[3]), int(sys.argv[4]))); sys.exit(0)
    elif mode == "conf-test": sys.exit(conf_test(setup))
    elif mode == "wait-test": sys.exit(conf_test(wait_setup))
    elif mode == "check-test": sys.exit(check_test(float(sys.argv[2]) if len(sys.argv) > 2 else 0))
    else:
        print(__doc__); sys.exit(0 if mode in ("-h", "--help") else 2)
